course: dictionary
lesson: 5398
title: A story word and a real animal
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words come from stories, and some come from the real world. Rudyard Kipling wrote «How the Leopard Got His Spots» and spelled one animal «Koodoo». Today we write kudu. Context helps: the story says the Koodoo lived on the sandy-yellow High Veldt and later hid in the stripy shadows of the forest.
explain: The clues are in the sentences. «...the Eland and the Koodoo grew darker, with little wavy grey lines on their backs like bark on a tree trunk» tells you the Koodoo has a grey-brown body with lines on it. Those lines help it blend in among the trees, so Kipling is describing an animal that is hard to see.
explain: Check your guess by swapping in the real name. The Koodoo is the same animal as the kudu, and kudu is a real African antelope. Eland and hartebeest are also real antelopes, so the story uses real animals.
fact: A koodoo is Kipling's spelling of kudu.
fact: A kudu is a large African antelope.
fact: The male kudu has long spiral horns, which are twisted like a corkscrew.
fact: Both male and female kudus have thin white stripes on a grey-brown body.
fact: The stripes are camouflage that helps the kudu hide from predators, and they developed over a very long time through evolution.
exercise: Kipling spelled one animal «Koodoo». What do we write today? | Kudo | *Kudu | Kidoo | Koodle
exercise: «...with little wavy grey lines on their backs like bark on a tree trunk». What do the wavy grey lines look like? | Stripes on a zebra | *Bark on a tree trunk | Spots on a leopard | Waves on the sea
exercise: Why do the stripes help a kudu? | *It helps it hide from predators | It makes it easy to spot from far away | It shows how old it is | It helps it sing loudly
test: What is a kudu? | *A large African antelope | A type of bird | A kind of tree | A small furry insect
test: Which part of the male kudu is long and twisted? | Its tail | Its hooves | Its ears | *Its horns
exam: Where can you find out for certain what koodoo means? | In the spots of a leopard | Nowhere, because it is a made-up word | *In a dictionary, under kudu | In the sandy-yellow veldt
